                                    ------
                                    IO Usage
                                    ------
                                    Jay Liang
                                    ------
                                     2007/08/14

Writing a RSS 2.0 feed
      
      Writing a feed is easy after the Channel object is built. This builds a RSS 2.0 feed xml file.
Note that since every feed has only one channel, the top level 'rss' element is automatically generated
for you, all you need to do is to pass in the channel object:

+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
	
  Channel c = new Channel()
				...
				
  FeedWriter w = new FeedWriter("yarfraw.xml"));
  w.writeChannel(c);

+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+      

	Currently the writer uses JAXB to write the xml file. You can inject your own error event handler
to the writer is case you need to handle the event differently:

+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
	
  w.writeChannel(c, new ValidationEventHandler(){
	public boolean handleEvent(ValidationEvent event) {
	//Process the event
		return false;
	}  
  });

+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Add/Remove items 
	
	Sometimes you may want simply add a couple of items to an existing feed with out modifying the 
top level channel. You can use the 'Appender' to do that. Internally, the appender reads in the feed, 
modifies it, and then write it back to the file:

+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
   
    File f = new File("yarfraw.xml");
    FeedAppender a = new FeedAppender(f);
    a.addItem(item);
    a.removeItem(2);

+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Reading a RSS 2.0 feed

	You can use the reader class to read any RSS 2.0 feed.
	
+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
   
    FeedReader r = new FeedReader( "yarfraw.xml");
    Channel c = r.readChannel();
   
+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
	
	The reader is capable of reading a feed from a remote http url:
	
+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
   
    FeedReader reader = new FeedReader(new HttpURL("http://somewhere.com/rssfeed.xml"));
    Channel c = r.readChannel();
    
    //check whether a reader is reading from a remote server
    reader.isRemoteRead();
   
+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
		
	Similarly, you can add your own event handler:
	
+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
   
  r.readChannel(new ValidationEventHandler(){
      public boolean handleEvent(ValidationEvent event) {
      	//Process the event
        return false;
      }
  });
   
+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+

  For more details, I encourage to take a look at the {{{apidocs/index.html}Javadoc}}.
  Also check out the {{{faq.html}FAQ}} section for more insights about this API. 
  
